  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of tropical moisture characterized by \n2\"+ PWATs and dewpoints in the 70s is forecast to get pulled \nnorth through the southeastern CONUS over the weekend to the \neast of the northward-advancing remnants of TC Isaias. At the \nsurface, a warm front will trudge north along the southeastern\ncoastline, likely with a weak, non-tropical area of low \npressure developing along it. Along and ahead of the warm front,\na strong southeasterly LLJ will develop, supporting increasing \nWAA and moisture transport into the area with a shield of \nmoderate to heavy rain developing across the coastal Carolinas. \nIt's expected that this initial swath of precip should support \n1-3\" of rainfall for much of ENC. Along and just behind the \nwarm front, the precip will likely become more banded and\ncontain some embedded convective elements. It's these bands \nthat will pose the most intense rainfall rates, as well as the \ngreatest risk of flooding. Convective guidance suggests rainfall\namounts as high as 4-6\"+ are possible if those bands manage to \nreach the coast. As is often the case in these scenarios, it's \nunclear how far inland the instability axis and, consequently, \nthe heaviest rates will get. \n\nRainfall exceedance probabilities from the NBM have stabilized \nfrom last night, still suggesting a 20-30% of seeing more than \n4\" rain focused along the southern coastline roughly along the \nNC24 corridor including Morehead City and Jacksonville. The 00z\nsuite of guidance has also stayed consistent with only minor\nfluctuations in overall totals. WPC continues to keep much of\nour area in a Slight (Level 2 of 4) risk of excessive rainfall\nand flash flooding. After collaboration with neighboring\noffices, consideration of a Flood Watch has been deferred to the\nnext shift with heaviest rainfall likely not until Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Steady increase in moisture through the weekend\npeaks on Sunday contributing to an increase in instability \nacross portions of ENC, greatest south of Highway 264. Factors \nlike cloud cover will determine how much we end up getting, but \nensemble averages suggest CAPEs as high as 1000+ J/kg in some \nspots. While low- level forcing is in question, it's a setup \nthat bears watching for a conditional severe thunderstorm risk.\nIf a stronger storm develops, the environment would be favorable\nfor rotating cells and a tornado threat given elevated SRH along\nthe front.\n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with a lower chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms by Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strengthening onshore winds, building seas, and\nhigher astronomical tides may support some coastal impacts this\nweekend. There remains uncertainty on the magnitude of impacts,\nespecially given the shorter-duration of the stronger winds, as\nwell as what should be a weaker swell compared to the recent \nNor'easter. With that in mind, there will be an increased risk \nof rip currents and large waves, at a minimum. High Surf\nAdvisory remains in effect between Ocracoke and Oregon Inlets\nfocused on Sunday. Additionally, some of the most vulnerable \nlocations along the Outer Banks may see at least some minor \nissues, especially with Sunday morning's high tide (7-9am). \nThere may also be some minor coastal flooding around sounds and \ntidal waterways around the Crystal Coast due to the higher \nastronomical tides.",
